// SOS CHILD: THE DONATED PROCESS (sawos design 4 — M3 unit 3's money shot).
//
// **THE FIRST SOS PROCESS THAT IS NEITHER ROOT NOR DOOMED.** Every child before
// this unit received `NO_HANDLE` in its first argument register and could do
// exactly one thing — die trying (see `child-fault`). This one is handed its OWN
// Process handle there, drains the boot set its launcher furnished, and ends
// itself with a code. The whole of unit 3 is what makes those three sentences
// writable.
//
// WHAT IT ASSERTS, and every one of these is a property of `give`:
//
//   - THREE RECORDS arrive, which is exactly what the launcher gave.
//   - THEY ARRIVE IN GIVE ORDER — tags 3, 5, 7, accumulated as DIGITS into
//     `357` so that one number carries the ORDER and not only the set. A kernel
//     that delivered them out of order would produce a different number.
//   - THEIR KINDS are Memory, Memory, Process — `001` accumulated, so `1`.
//   - `Drained` STAYS `Drained`: the second ask after the loop hands nothing
//     back, which is the claim that the cursor only advances and that a boot
//     set cannot be collected twice.
//
// AND IT REPORTS THEM WITH ITS EXIT CODE, because it has no other voice. A
// donated child holds no System handle — nothing mints a givable one in v1 — so
// it cannot print, and its LAUNCHER cannot read its §8 status word either: root
// gave the one Process handle away, which is design 4 D-3's "one handle, one
// choice" seen from the far side. The kernel's own `SOS: process exit:` line is
// therefore the oracle, and root never has to witness anything. That is the
// proof the unit is actually about.
//
// **THE ONE HAZARD IN THIS PROGRAM, and it is the design's rather than this
// file's.** The handle in a0 and the record tagged 7 are THE SAME WORD: starting
// with a boot tag resolves the tag, it does not consume the record, so the child
// meets its own Process handle twice — once as a register and once as an
// iterator entry. Two live wrappers over one word would mean two releases, and
// the second is a `BadHandle` fault the program did nothing to deserve. So this
// file TAKES the Process out of its record and keeps it for the rest of `_start`,
// beside the adopted one, and ends with `exit` — which never returns, so neither
// wrapper ever runs its deinit and the word is closed exactly once, by the
// kernel's teardown. `sos` cannot check this for you: one construction is a
// register read and the other an iterator entry, and nothing connects them.
//
// It names no architecture and would build for either profile unchanged; the
// only per-machine thing about it is the linker script the manifest points at.

import sos.{Process, BootHandle, BootHandleKind, SosStatus}

/// What the launcher's config says it gave, in the order it gave it. This side
/// of the contract is the child's manifest of expectations; the other side is
/// `give-boot-drain`'s three `give` calls. THE TAGS ARE THE ONLY THING THE TWO
/// SHARE — the kernel supplies no meaning for any of them.
static WANT_RECORDS: UInt = 3
static WANT_TAGS: UInt = 357
static WANT_KINDS: UInt = 1

/// What it ends with when the boot set was not what the config promised. A
/// number that could not be a record count, so the transcript's `code=` field
/// distinguishes the two outcomes on sight.
static EXIT_MISMATCH: UInt = 99

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    // THE ADOPTION. `boot_handle` is the word the kernel resolved out of this
    // process's own boot set at `start(donating:)`, so it is a handle the kernel
    // handed over rather than a number anybody guessed — the same blessed
    // crossing `System(boot_handle:)` is for root.
    let me = Process(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // The record tagged 7 names the very word above (see the header). Taking it
    // here is what stops the record from releasing that word when it drops.
    var donated: Process? = None

    var count: UInt = 0
    var tags: UInt = 0
    var kinds: UInt = 0
    var going = true
    while going {
        match me.boot_handle_next() {
            case Ok(h) -> {
                var rec = move h
                tags = tags * 10 + rec.tag
                kinds = kinds * 10 + (rec.kind as UInt)
                match rec.kind {
                    case Process -> { donated = rec.take_process() },
                    // A region this child was given and does not want is a
                    // capability it declines: the record drops at the end of
                    // this arm and releases it, which is drop-is-release doing
                    // exactly what design 3 built it for.
                    case Memory -> {},
                }
                count = count + 1
            },
            case Err(_) -> { going = false },
        }
    }

    // AND IT STAYS DRAINED. The cursor only advances, so a boot set is collected
    // once — which matters more for a child than it did for root, since these
    // records are capabilities a launcher may have given away exactly once.
    let still_drained = match me.boot_handle_next() {
        case Ok(_) -> false,
        case Err(_) -> true,
    }

    // The brackets are load-bearing: a binary expression does not wrap in Saw
    // unless something already encloses it (DF-172d).
    let as_promised = (count == WANT_RECORDS && tags == WANT_TAGS
                       && kinds == WANT_KINDS && still_drained)
    if not as_promised {
        let _ = me.exit(EXIT_MISMATCH)
    }

    // THE EXIT CODE IS THE DRAIN COUNT, and this call does not return — which is
    // also what keeps the two wrappers over one word harmless (see the header).
    let _ = me.exit(count)
}
